              <td style="padding:16px;width:100%;vertical-align:middle">
                <h2>Research</h2>
				  
				<p>
				<br>
			 	My research focuses on the development of advanced polymer electrolyte membranes (PEMs), which are critical components of fuel cells and water electrolysis systems.
				My work aims to address key challenges in the hydrogen energy sector by developing high-performance, durable, and cost-effective membrane materials.
				</br>
			  
				</br>
				My expertise lies in the design, synthesis, and functionalization of novel polymeric materials. I specialize in tailoring the morphology and microstructure of polymer
			  	membranes to optimize their ionic conductivity, chemical stability, and mechanical strength, which are essential for achieving efficient and durable electrochemical 
			  	performance. My research also involves advanced characterization techniques to investigate the physicochemical, structural, and morphological properties of these 
			  	materials at both the molecular and macroscopic levels. 
				</br>
			
				<br>
				Prior to my postdoctoral appointment, I earned my Ph.D. in Polymer Chemistry from Incheon National University, where I established a strong foundation in polymer science and 
				advanced functional materials. Throughout my academic career, I have maintained a strong commitment to advancing sustainable and green energy technologies, particularly 
				hydrogen-based energy systems. I have published my research in reputable scientific journals and actively participated in international conferences, fostering academic 
				collaboration and contributing to the advancement of clean energy research.
				</br>
			  
				<br>
				With a strong passion for sustainable energy solutions and a robust technical background in polymeric
				materials and electrochemical technologies, I aspire to contribute to the global transition toward a low-carbon energy future by developing innovative membrane materials 
				that can enhance the efficiency, durability, and scalability of hydrogen technologies. ome papers are <span class="highlight">highlighted below</span>.
				</br>	
